// A String const is exactly as expressive as a String literal, and no more.
//
// Joining two of them at compile time would work perfectly well — the answer is just a longer
// literal. It is refused anyway, because `+` on Strings MEANS allocate everywhere else in
// Burxt: it needs a region, and `region` is the word that tells a reader where memory lives.
// A `const` that allocated nothing while spelled the same way as the operator that does would
// teach the reader that `+` on Strings sometimes means nothing at all.
const NAME: String = "burxt";
const GREETING: String = "hello, " + NAME;
print(GREETING);
